## III. FAILURE MODE 1 — AUTHORITY OSCILLATION AND THE HYSTERESIS SOLUTION

The most dangerous moment in any authority-sharing system is not a clear emergency. It is the ambiguous threshold.

Consider: life-support sensors fluctuating between 29% and 31% reserve capacity. At 30%, the decision topology escalates authority from Chain 2 to Chain 1 — AI-Primary to Human Judgment. But the sensors keep oscillating. Authority escalates. Conditions stabilize. Authority de-escalates. Then it oscillates again. Within minutes, human crew members receive conflicting authority signals, the AI is executing and then suspending actions mid-sequence, and nobody in the room can answer the simplest operational question: *who is in charge right now?*

This is the Authority Oscillation problem. It is not hypothetical. Threshold-crossing instability is a known failure mode in industrial control systems on Earth. In a sealed lunar habitat, it is a life-safety catastrophe.

**The architectural solution is hysteresis — directional locks with deliberate dampening.**

In engineering, hysteresis means a system responds differently depending on the direction of change, not just the current value. A thermostat with hysteresis turns on at 18°C and turns off at 22°C — it doesn't toggle every time the temperature crosses 20°.

Applied to the decision topology, the rule is asymmetric: **escalating authority upward to humans is fast; returning authority downward to AI requires a deliberate, stable confirmation period.** Specifically:

Once an anomaly triggers escalation from Chain 2 or Chain 3 to Chain 1 (Human Judgment), authority cannot revert to the previous chain until: (a) a minimum stability window has elapsed — measured not in seconds but in verified stable readings across multiple independent sensor arrays; (b) a human authority holder has issued explicit confirmation, not merely passive non-objection; and (c) the escalation event has been logged and its cause formally categorized as resolved or suppressed.

This asymmetry is intentional. The cost of a human being in charge of a decision the AI could handle is inefficiency. The cost of the AI reclaiming authority from a human during an unresolved crisis is potential catastrophe. The dampening field accepts the first cost to prevent the second.

The dampening parameters — minimum stability windows, confirmation protocols, sensor redundancy thresholds — are not universal. They are tuned per decision category. Atmospheric authority has different dampening parameters than medical diagnostics, which have different parameters than structural integrity. Designing those category-specific parameters is one of the architect's most technically demanding responsibilities.

