## IV. FAILURE MODE 2 — HUMAN COMPETENCE DECAY AND THE ACTIVE SHADOWING PROTOCOL

Key human competencies may be shifting from information-processing skills to interpersonal and organizational skills as AI agents enter the workforce. [stanford](https://futureofwork.saltlab.stanford.edu)

In a colony where AI successfully manages 90% of operational decisions, this shift is not gradual — it accelerates. And it creates a trap.

The trap is this: a colony that runs well under AI management is a colony whose human crew members are, day by day, losing the cognitive muscle memory required to run it themselves. Automation bias sets in. Analysis paralysis develops. When Chain 1 authority is finally invoked — during a genuine existential crisis, precisely the moment when human judgment is most needed — the humans in the room are operating decision-making capabilities they haven't exercised in months.

Long-duration exposure to conditions of isolation and confinement leads to immune dysfunction, cognitive impairment, and anxiety. [ScienceDirect](https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2214552425000604) Add automation-induced cognitive atrophy to that baseline and you have a crew whose Chain 1 capacity is degraded exactly when the colony most depends on it.

**The architectural solution is the Active Shadowing Protocol — mandatory, daily, covert.**

The protocol works as follows. In parallel with every AI-managed Chain 2 decision above a defined complexity threshold, designated human crew members run a silent, real-time shadow decision process. They receive the same sensor data the AI receives. They make their own decision, independently, before the AI's decision is revealed to them. The AI acts. The human's shadow decision is logged and compared to the AI's outcome.

The protocol is not a performance review of the AI. It is a training regimen for the humans. Its explicit purpose is to keep human decision-making capacity calibrated against real operational conditions, using actual colony data, at a cognitive difficulty level that reflects genuine crisis demands.

Three design principles govern Active Shadowing:

**It must be covert for some participants, some of the time.** Crew members cannot always know when their shadow decisions are being logged for competence tracking versus when they are being used to evaluate their performance. Known evaluation changes behavior. Genuine calibration requires that some shadow sessions feel like routine cognitive maintenance.

**It must escalate in difficulty.** Easy scenarios maintain baseline competency. The protocol must include regular injection of high-complexity, multi-variable scenarios — atmospheric failures cascading into medical emergencies — to maintain the high-stress decision capacity that routine operations will never exercise.

**It must be protected from being treated as optional.** In a busy colony under operational pressure, the temptation to defer or abbreviate cognitive maintenance protocols is constant. The Architect must design Active Shadowing as a structural requirement tied to Chain 1 authorization status — crew members who have not completed their Active Shadowing allotment within a rolling time window lose Chain 1 authority until they do.

This last point is the hardest politically and the most important architecturally. The right to make the colony's highest-stakes decisions must be contingent on demonstrated cognitive readiness, verified against real operational conditions, on a continuous basis.

