## V. FAILURE MODE 3 — THE EXPLAINABILITY TRAP AND INTENT-BASED COMMUNICATION

Most explainable AI frameworks share a fundamental design error: they are built around the question *why did the AI do that?*

That question is the wrong question under crisis conditions.

When a life-support AI makes an unexpected atmospheric adjustment at 3am and the on-duty crew member needs to decide in under 20 seconds whether to override or confirm, a detailed causal explanation of the AI's decision process is not just unhelpful — it is actively harmful. It consumes cognitive bandwidth the crew member does not have. It floods a decision space that needs to be compressed, not expanded. Current human-in-the-loop paradigms often lead to cognitive overload and decision-making bottlenecks in complex, high-stakes environments. [arxiv](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2510.24030) Conventional explainability frameworks are a primary driver of that overload.

**The architectural solution is a shift from diagnostic explanation to intent-forward display.**

The interface paradigm changes from "Why I did this" to "What I intend to do next."

In practice, this means: rather than presenting the crew member with a diagnostic readout of the AI's reasoning, the system displays a single glanceable panel showing three elements only:

**The AI's projected next three actions**, expressed in plain operational language. Not "executing atmospheric compensation subroutine 7-C" but "increasing oxygen flow in Module 3 / reducing CO₂ scrubber cycle in Module 1 / venting coolant loop B."

**The expected outcome of those actions**, expressed as a single status indicator: nominal, managed, or critical. Not a probability distribution. One indicator.

**A veto mechanism**, accessible in under two seconds, requiring a single deliberate physical confirmation (not accidental activation) that freezes the AI's next action and escalates to Chain 1.

The design philosophy underlying this interface is that human oversight in a crisis is not about understanding — it is about interception. The crew member's job at 3am is not to understand why the AI is doing what it's doing. It is to notice if what the AI intends to do next looks wrong, and to be able to stop it before it happens. Intent-forward display is optimized for that specific cognitive task.

The veto-not-override design is deliberate. A veto freezes and escalates. An override replaces. Veto is safer because it does not require the human to immediately know what to do instead — it only requires them to know that the AI's intended action is wrong. What to do instead can be worked out in the time the veto buys.

