## VII. FAILURE MODE 5 — GEOPOLITICAL REALITY AND THE LEGAL-TECHNICAL MAPPING LAYER

The decision topology, as originally designed, has a sovereignty blind spot.

The Artemis Accords, now with more than 60 signatories, provide a common set of principles to enhance the governance of the civil exploration and use of outer space as many countries and private companies conduct missions and operations around the Moon. [NASA](https://www.nasa.gov/humans-in-space/artemis/) But the Accords are principles, not specifications. They establish that nations cooperate. They do not specify what happens when an American-designed AI system, operating on pre-authorized autonomous authority, overrides a directive issued by a European astronaut during a crisis in a module funded by a Japanese space agency contract.

Under current space law, liability follows the launching state. If that AI system's autonomous decision causes harm — or is perceived to have caused harm — the legal chain of accountability runs back to the nation that launched and operates the system, not to the colony's internal authority structure. In a multi-national colony, every autonomous AI decision above a certain consequence threshold is simultaneously a technical event and a geopolitical event.

A decision topology that does not account for this will produce crises not just operational but diplomatic — and diplomatic crises in a colony 384,000 kilometers from Earth, with 14-second communication delays and no ability to convene a treaty negotiation, are existential threats in their own right.

**The solution is a Legal-Technical Mapping Layer embedded in Layer 3 of the Colony Decision Constitution.**

This layer does three things:

**First, it maps every authority chain to its applicable jurisdictional framework.** For every category of decision in the topology, the layer specifies which nation's law governs liability, which treaty framework applies, and what the escalation path is if a dispute arises between crew members operating under different national legal frameworks.

**Second, it pre-negotiates crisis authority protocols across signatories.** Before the colony launches, the Architect — working alongside space lawyers and diplomatic representatives — negotiates and formalizes agreement on pre-authorized AI authority parameters that all participating nations accept as compliant with their legal obligations. This is not a minor document. It is a treaty-level instrument, and producing it is part of the architect's professional scope.

**Third, it includes real-time compliance flags in the intent-forward interface.** When the AI's next intended action crosses a threshold with jurisdictional implications — a medical intervention on a non-consenting crew member, an emergency authority escalation that affects resources a specific nation has treaty rights over — the interface flags it. Not with a legal brief. With a single indicator: *Jurisdictional Notice.* The crew member knows to document the decision explicitly, and the action is logged with an automatic notification to the relevant parties.

The critical design constraint is that the legal-technical mapping layer must never slow down crisis response below safe operating thresholds. It exists to make compliance tractable, not to impose compliance reviews in the middle of emergencies. The pre-negotiation work done before colony launch is precisely what allows the real-time layer to be a flag, not a gate.

